      "latency_ms": 30061.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1260,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water can form droplets and flow.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter on Earth:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water freezes, the molecules slow down and lock into a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we are most familiar with. The molecules are close together but can move past one another, allowing water to take the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Water Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape the liquid and float into the air.\n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nWater is excellent at dissolving other substances. Because of its polar nature, it can pull apart the bonds of many ionic and polar molecules. This is why it is called the \"universal solvent.\" It is the primary way nutrients and minerals are transported through the bodies of plants and animals.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, clouds, rain, glaciers, and deep underground.\n\n### 5. Importance to Life\nWater is essential for life as we know it. It acts as a chemical reactant in photosynthesis and respiration, a medium for biological reactions, a lubricant for joints, and a way to regulate body temperature through sweating. Without water, life as we know it could not exist."
